@soispoke The fix seems correct, after the calldata floor landed, the old `sum(gas_limit) − total_gas_used` refund does return part of the floor charge, and the escrow/settlement split with the invariants block is the clean way out. Also good that` TXPARAM(0x06)` prose now matches the introspection table ("basefee=max"), which was ambiguous before.

One issue: "A valid transaction has `effective_gas_price <= max_fee_per_gas and blob_base_fee <= max_fee_per_blob_gas`", the field rules require `max_fee_per_blob_gas = 0` when `blob_versioned_hashes` is empty, and `blob_base_fee` is never zero, so as written this invalidates every blob-less frame transaction. I think the blob condition needs to apply only when `len(blob_versioned_hashes) > 0`.

One clarification worth maybe a sentence: with #11940's gross per-frame receipts, the frame receipts won't sum to `charged_gas` (floor and refund are transaction-level), stating that explicitly will possibly save every implementer the same double-take.

@nerolation `charged_gas`, `calldata_tokens`, and `tokens_in` are defined in EIP-8141's Gas Accounting section, so those terms should remain: https://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/blob/master/EIPS/eip-8141.md#gas-accounting

EIP-7623 counts a zero byte as one token and a nonzero byte as four tokens. Using `len(nonce_calldata)` would introduce a different floor rule for the nonce fields. I would keep the EIP-7623 treatment unless we choose a fixed byte surcharge.

I agree with adding EIP-7623 to `requires` and specifying where the nonce data enters the execution branch.

Does this also need to cover standalone `APPROVE_EXECUTION`? The current text discusses `APPROVE_PAYMENT` while including `sender_approved`, which can be set separately.

Please define each scope:

- `APPROVE_EXECUTION` sets `sender_approved`;
- `APPROVE_PAYMENT` sets `payer`, consumes the nonce, and collects the maximum cost;
- `APPROVE_EXECUTION_AND_PAYMENT` performs both.

Successful approval effects should not be part of frame or atomic batch rollback snapshots. They remain part of the enclosing transaction and block state. They survive a later frame revert or atomic batch rollback, but are discarded if the transaction is invalid or the block is removed by a reorg.

@AnkushinDaniil 

1. What happens when `APPROVE` runs in an inner call and the enclosing frame then reverts? "Outside the individual-frame revert journal" says the effects stick. "Survive a later frame revert" says only subsequent frames are meant. Pick one. I'd say the effects should only apply if the frame that ran `APPROVE` completes successfully, otherwise a reverted frame leaves behind a nonce increment and a payer debit while everything else it did is undone, and implementations have to exempt individual writes from the frame's revert instead of applying effects at frame boundaries.

2. Add a warning about the batch rollback. The nonce increment and cost collection are plain state writes. If a client implements atomic-batch rollback as "restore a snapshot taken at batch start" (the obvious way) an in-batch `APPROVE_PAYMENT` gets its debit and nonce silently reverted, while payer survives as a protocol variable. The tx then passes the `payer != None` check and the refund at the end pays the payer money that was never collected. I hit this in our prototype in Nethermind. One sentence saying these writes must be exempted from (or re-applied after) the rollback would save the next implementer the same bug.

@AnkushinDaniil yes, I wanted to make a similar point. To address this, I updated the formula to use the form `tx_gas_limit - tx_unused_gas` when computing the quotient.

--

After thinking about it more, I don't love that the receipt `gas_used` doesn't match the refund adjusted amount and/or that the sum of `gas_used` fields in the receipt does not equal the actual `gas_used` by the transaction. I think this is something we should think more about, but I'm okay with this for now while we continue to evaluate options. I think the refund mechanism is now sufficiently defined.

